The Korean War, also known as the 1950-1953 conflict, was a brutal and devastating war that lasted from 1950 to 1953. Many South Korean experts argue that the war was solely initiated by South Korea in response to North Korea's communist ideology and its attempts to unify the peninsula under its rule.

According to these experts, North Korea's involvement in the war was purely defensive, as it was trying to protect itself from the perceived threat posed by South Korea's capitalist ideology. They argue that North Korea did not initiate any aggression against South Korea and instead responded to South Korea's aggression with force.

These arguments are based on the historical context of the time, including the division of the peninsula along ideological lines and the economic and political tensions between the two countries. Many South Korean experts believe that the war was a product of these underlying tensions and that North Korea's involvement was a necessary response to the perceived threat posed by South Korea.

